timestamp=2026-11-09T09:27:43.040Z level=INFO run=ce357dcf message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# Calibration says value_column_name is the first variant (\"power_reading \" with space) - which is the first of the two in the header. But the data in that first column looks odd/constant in parts? For D, mostly 80.0 which happens to equal calibration_offset - that is suspicious. Calibration offset is applied as (recorded - true). Also values that become negative after offset must be dropped.\n# If recorded is 80.0 and offset is 80.0, then true value is 0.0. That fits with night-time readings being 0 after offset. That suggests the first column (power_reading, the left one) is the actual recorded value column that calibration refers to. And power_reading_1 (second) might be some other field.\n# Let us verify. For example, look at a row where power_reading_1 is larger. At the end of D: last row had power_reading ~3291.12 and power_reading_1 ~42.12. If reading_type is power in kW, those are very different magnitudes. Also installed capacities vary - look at farms.csv: FARM_D is Kowhai Solar Plant, Pacific/Auckland - installed_capacity_kw is 1000. So peak power ~1000 kW is plausible. 3291 kW would be way over. 80 kW could be plausible at certain times? Or maybe not. But 80.0 constant in many rows is odd. \n# Also compare against calibration_offset. For FARM_D, offset is 80.0. That means recorded value is true + offset. So if true is 0 (night), recorded is 80.0. That matches perfectly with the first column being the recorded sensor value. So the first column is the raw recorded value. The second column (power_reading_1) is something else (maybe another sensor or computed field). That is why calibration points to \"power_reading \" (first one).\n# That changes everything - we must pick the column that matches the value_column_name from calibration, not just any plausible value column.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
