timestamp=2026-11-09T09:27:52.944Z level=INFO run=ce357dcf message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# Similarly for FARM_F: output (first) is often 0. Calibration offset is 0.0. And output_1 varies - if output is the recorded value and offset is 0, then true is output. So if true should be 0 at night, recorded is 0. That also matches the first column. That means the first of the duplicated columns is the one we need.\n# So in our deduplication, when we see the same column name appearing twice, we need to know which one is the \"original\" position? Or better, preserve the first occurrence's semantics. Or alternatively, when the file has the exact same column name written twice, pandas will typically create .1 or _1 for the second - but which physical column in the file corresponds to which? In CSV reading by position with names, the names are assigned positionally. So position of the first \"power_reading \" in header gives rise to the first \"power_reading\" in our cleaned names list? Let us trace: header cols in order: ..., 'power_reading' (from first occurrence), 'power_reading' (from second occurrence) -> we dedupe by appending _1 to second. So the first in our resulting names corresponds to the first occurrence in the file (leftmost). And that first occurrence is the one the calibration says \"value_column_name\" is \"power_reading \" (exactly matching). So the first occurrence is the one we need. That means we should take the column without suffix (the first one), or the one that matches the calibration's column name before our normalization. That is correct.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
