timestamp=2026-11-09T10:00:34.085Z level=INFO run=ce357dcf message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# So for \"29,0200\" our heuristic treats it as decimal (comma, 4 digits after -> but 4 > 3? 4 digits after comma, len is 4, so len(parts[1]) <= 3 is false; so it goes to else and removes commas -> becomes \"290200\" which is 290200.0. That might be wrong if it meant 29.02. Or might be correct depending on data representation.\n# But let us look at what makes sense for the actual data values in context. For FARM_E, earlier when we saw the corrupted parsing we got different pieces; but in the proper UTF-16LE semicolon-split form, the values are like '29,0200', '56,8961' etc. Are these measurements? Or coded? For power/energy values, 290200 is a very large number if it's watts. Or 29.02 kW. Looking at other columns and the scale - for example irradiance_w_m2 around 800+ in some cases, panel values, etc. These numbers are in a similar magnitude range as the other numeric fields in the same row format. So probably they are the actual values with comma as thousand separator or just formatted. That gives large integers - not matching typical small readings. That can't be.\n# Alternatively, maybe comma is decimal and there are exactly 4 decimal places consistently? Like 29,0200 means 29.0200 (i.e. 29.02). That gives values in reasonable range. That fits.\n# Let us look at the other numbers in the same row. For example, '56,8961' - if decimal comma, 56.8961. That could be plausible for irradiance or other fields. Also '86,7021' -> 86.7 something. That seems plausible.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
