timestamp=2026-11-09T10:00:45.148Z level=INFO run=ce357dcf message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# So comma is decimal separator here (European style) with 4 decimal places. That changes how we parse. We need to detect the format per file or per value? Or try both approaches.\n# Better approach: if the string has both comma and dot, decide by position (last one is decimal). If only comma, assume it's decimal separator (not thousands) when followed by 3+ digits? Or just always treat comma as decimal if it appears and is followed by 3 or more digits in a certain pattern? Or look at the pattern across all numbers in the file - if most numbers have 4 digits after comma, it's likely decimal.\n# For FARM_E, all numeric fields have 4 digits after comma. That suggests comma is decimal. So in general, for this dataset, maybe comma is decimal separator in some files. Let us look at FARM_B's raw values - earlier FARM_B was fine with dot. Let us check a FARM_B data line.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
