timestamp=2026-11-09T10:01:51.711Z level=INFO run=ce357dcf message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# But for \"29,0200\" with European interpretation (comma as decimal) gives 29.0200 = 29.02. With thousands interpretation (comma removed) gives 290200. For typical sensor readings in these datasets, 29.02 is reasonable, 290200 is not. So European format is more plausible here.\n# But for \"1,234.56\" (both present), comma before dot - comma as thousands, dot as decimal. Our earlier logic: s.rfind(',') > s.rfind('.') ? replace both : replace comma only. \"1,234.56\" - comma at pos 1, dot at 5, rfind comma (1) vs rfind dot (5): comma is before dot (rfind comma 1, rfind dot 5, so 1 > 5 is false). So we replace comma only -> \"1234.56\". Correct.\n# For \"1.234,56\" - dot at 1, comma at 5: rfind comma (5) > rfind dot (1) is true -> replace dot with empty, comma with dot -> \"1234.56\". Correct.\n# So that logic is fine. But for \"29,0200\" (only comma, no dot): len(parts)==2 and len(parts[1]) <= 3? parts[1] is \"0200\" has 4 chars. So condition false, we remove commas -> get 290200. Wrong in this case.\n# So the heuristic about number of digits after comma is flawed for 4-decimal formatting. Another heuristic: if there are exactly 3 digits after comma, often thousands; if 4, often decimal in some systems? Or just look at the magnitude of typical values in the dataset.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
