timestamp=2026-11-09T10:06:56.118Z level=INFO run=ce357dcf message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# The meter_value is -50000.0 (in European format, so parse gives -50000.0). Our current parse_num_smart should parse '-50.000,0000' correctly with the European style try - replace dots, replace comma with dot: s.replace('.', '') gives '-5000', then replace comma with dot on '-5000'? Wait look at our implementation: for European style try we do c = s.replace('.', '').replace(',', '.') So '-50.000,0000' becomes '-50000.0000' (dots removed first: 50.000 becomes 50000? \"50.000\" after replace '.' with '' gives \"50000\", then replace ',' with '.' gives \"50000.\" no wait look: s is '-50.000,0000'. s.replace('.', '') removes all dots -> '-50000,0000'. Then replace(',', '.') -> '-50000.0000'. So float gives -50000.0. Correct.\n# But our read_production_robust is parsing FARM_E as comma-separated with our normalization? Let us check how it parsed FARM_E's columns - earlier it gave shape (291, 17) with meter_value column, but the values got split incorrectly? Because we normalized by replacing tabs/semicolons with commas globally. That corrupts fields when commas are part of the number formatting or when semicolons are the real delimiters mixed with commas in numbers. We need a better parser that respects the actual delimiter per file.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
