timestamp=2026-11-09T10:12:34.825Z level=INFO run=ce357dcf message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# The results are similar after dropping duplicates - probably no duplicates or minimal impact. \n# Let us review the rules again: timestamps that cannot be parsed reliably must be ignored - we already skip rows where ts_utc is NaN. Good.\n# Negative values after calibration offset applied must be dropped - implemented. Good.\n# Readings during completed maintenance windows must be excluded - we do that by converting local window to check against ts_local. Let us verify for FARM_F specifically (Vistula Photovoltaics has completed cleaning on March 15 12:00-14:00 local). FARM_F's timezone is Europe/Warsaw (from farms.csv). On 2024-03-15 in Europe/Warsaw, 12:00-14:00 local is what UTC? Warsaw is UTC+1 in standard time in March (CET, UTC+1; no DST switch mentioned). So 12:00-14:00 local is 11:00-13:00 UTC. That means readings with timestamps in 11:00-13:00 UTC on 2024-03-15 should be excluded. Let us check if our logic did that.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
